<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Bdnd (Bit.Bdnd) - a declarative drag-and-drop library for Blazor inspired by React DnD, with typed items, drop targets, custom drag layers, drag handles, sortable lists and native file drops." />

    @* Link previews: the card a chat client or a social site unfurls is the first impression a
       shared link makes. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Bdnd" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Bdnd - declarative drag and drop for Blazor" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="React DnD's model in Blazor: draggables, drop targets, custom drag layers, drag handles, nesting and native file drops - with the drag state in C#." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles. Bit.Bdnd ships no stylesheet: its script module is imported by the
       components on demand. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Bdnd.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    @* The "Within an iframe" page frames the Single Target page in an iframe marked data-bdnd-frame.
       In that frame a page shows its sample and none of the site around it (demo.css), the way React
       DnD's own iframe example frames just the dustbin. Decided before first paint, or the chrome
       flashes in the frame; a frame of another origin has no frameElement and gets the whole site. *@
    <script>try { if (window.frameElement && window.frameElement.hasAttribute('data-bdnd-frame')) document.documentElement.classList.add('bdnd-framed'); } catch (_) { }</script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="favicon.png" />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

<body>
    @* InteractiveWebAssembly rather than Auto or Server: dragover fires continuously during a drag,
       and on WebAssembly none of its handlers waits on a network round trip. The pages are still
       prerendered, so the first response carries them. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
